!deck: Programming
!type: Basic
!tags: Programming Regex

!id: a1XM6pkgR6
!front: What is a Unicode property class in a regex?
!back: A Unicode property class matches characters by a Unicode property, such as "letter", "decimal digit", "mark", or a script. The exact syntax varies, but the concept is property-based character matching instead of literal enumeration.
~~~

!id: OtRsO8vTTE
!front: Compare a Unicode general category with a Unicode script property.
!back:
- A general category classifies what kind of character something is, such as letter, number, punctuation, or mark.
- A script property classifies the writing system a character belongs to, such as Latin, Greek, or Cyrillic.

They answer different questions about a character.
~~~

!id: ReQJkqyJbF
!front: Why is `[A-Z]` not the same as "any uppercase letter" in Unicode text?
!back: `[A-Z]` names a small ASCII range. Unicode has uppercase letters outside that range, so a Unicode property such as "uppercase letter" is needed for the broader concept.
~~~

!id: rfMcAUY887
!front: Why can Unicode combining marks surprise a regex written for "one character"?
!back: A user-perceived character may be represented by multiple code points, such as a base letter followed by a combining mark. A regex token that consumes one code point may match only part of that visible character.
~~~

!id: 23yZRG0KAD
!front: Compare a Unicode code point with a grapheme cluster.
!back:
- A code point is a single Unicode scalar value.
- A grapheme cluster is what a user typically perceives as one character, possibly made from multiple code points.

Regex tokens that mean "one character" often operate below the grapheme-cluster level.
~~~

!id: gnfhovawPf
!front: Why does Unicode normalization matter for regex matching?
!back: The same displayed text may have multiple Unicode representations, such as one precomposed code point or a base character plus combining mark. A literal regex may match one representation but not the other unless the input is normalized or the pattern accounts for both.
~~~

!id: K7mmkkNNdN
!front: Why is `\b` especially delicate in Unicode-aware matching?
!back: Word boundaries depend on what counts as a word character. In Unicode text, letters, marks, digits, and connector characters can make that boundary more subtle than the ASCII intuition of letters, digits, and underscore.
~~~

!id: q2UBJtpn9o
!front: Compare case-insensitive matching with explicitly listing both letter cases.
!back:
- Case-insensitive matching asks the regex engine to treat case variants as equivalent under its case-folding rules.
- Explicit listing, such as `[Aa]`, names only the variants written in the pattern.

The difference matters most in Unicode, where case relationships can be more complex than ASCII uppercase/lowercase pairs.
~~~
